{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are generally too high versus realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward; apply a downward level shift (increasing with horizon).",
    "Horizon-dependent drift error: the model tends to build an upward drift/plateau in weeks 2–4 while the market trends down; dampen trend component and enforce stronger mean reversion after day ~7.",
    "Overshoot after local rebounds: when the forecast shows a rebound/spike (e.g., mid-horizon), realized moves are smaller and fade faster; cap rebound amplitude and shorten rebound duration.",
    "Underestimates drawdown speed: realized declines occur earlier and faster than forecast; pull forward negative moves (reduce lag) by shifting part of the forecasted decline into the first 1–2 weeks.",
    "Volatility overstatement in later horizon: forecast oscillations/spikes exceed realized variability; apply volatility shrinkage (smoothing) increasing with horizon."
  ]
}
